{"content":[{"c":["ESHBAAL"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["King Saul’s fourth son, who became Israel’s king after his father’s death. Eshbaal literally means “man of Baal,” or “Baal exists” (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.8.33","r":"1Chr.8.33","rawR":"1Chr.8.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.8.33","value":"1Chr.8.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 8:33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.9.39","r":"1Chr.9.39","rawR":"1Chr.9.39","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.9.39","value":"1Chr.9.39"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:39"},"). During the period of the judges and the early monarchy, many Hebrew names were compounded with “baal,” a word that can mean “master” or “possessor.” Later generations were reluctant to speak the name “baal,” so “bosheth” (shame) was substituted (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Hos.2.16-17","r":"Hos.2.16-17","rawR":"Hos.2.16-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hos.2.16-17","value":"Hos.2.16-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hos 2:16-17"},"). Thus, Eshbaal was altered to Ishbosheth (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.2.8","r":"2Sam.2.8","rawR":"2Sam.2.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.2.8","value":"2Sam.2.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 2:8"},"), which means “man of shame.” Perhaps later copyists changed the name in the book of Samuel because it was read aloud in synagogue services, whereas Chronicles was not."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["After the death of Saul and his older sons, Abner, commander of Saul’s army, installed Ishbosheth as Israel’s king (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.2.8-9","r":"2Sam.2.8-9","rawR":"2Sam.2.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.2.8-9","value":"2Sam.2.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 2:8-9"},"). Judah’s tribe, however, followed King David, who struggled with Ishbosheth for leadership of all the tribes. The conflict lasted a long time, but the house of David gradually overwhelmed the house of Saul (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.3.1","r":"2Sam.3.1","rawR":"2Sam.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.3.1","value":"2Sam.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1"},"). Abner deserted Ishbosheth and was murdered by Joab, one of David’s men (v ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.3.27","r":"2Sam.3.27","rawR":"2Sam.3.27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.3.27","value":"2Sam.3.27"}],"t":"ref","v":"27"},"), thus removing an important leader of Israel and causing the people to despair (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.1","r":"2Sam.4.1","rawR":"2Sam.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.1","value":"2Sam.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"},"). Soon afterward Ishbosheth was murdered by two of his captains (v ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.7","r":"2Sam.4.7","rawR":"2Sam.4.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.7","value":"2Sam.4.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"7"},"). Although David disapproved of the deaths of Abner and Ishbosheth, the last obstacles to his kingship over all the tribes had been removed."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=David_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"David_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"David_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"David"},"; ",{"href":"?item=IsraelHistoryof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"IsraelHistoryof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"IsraelHistoryof_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Israel, History of"},"; ",{"href":"?item=Saul_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Saul_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Saul_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Saul #2"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Eshbaal","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"1 Chr 8:33","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Chr.8.33","rawQueryValue":"1Chr.8.33","sourceRefExact":"1Chr.8.33"},{"anchorText":"9:39","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Chr.9.39","rawQueryValue":"1Chr.9.39","sourceRefExact":"1Chr.9.39"},{"anchorText":"Hos 2:16-17","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Hos.2.16-17","rawQueryValue":"Hos.2.16-17","sourceRefExact":"Hos.2.16-17"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 2:8","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.2.8","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.2.8","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.2.8"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 2:8-9","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.2.8-9","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.2.8-9","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.2.8-9"},{"anchorText":"3:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.3.1","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.3.1","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.3.1"},{"anchorText":"27","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.3.27","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.3.27","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.3.27"},{"anchorText":"4:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.4.1","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.4.1","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.4.1"},{"anchorText":"7","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.4.7","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.4.7","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.4.7"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d2f2b532d5bef2855f0875c966a7cebdd26bda0cd30ff3058abdb5c741f1d89e","sourceItemName":"Eshbaal","sourceItemOrder":236,"sourceMember":"Articles/E.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eshbaal","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Eshbaal","title":"Eshbaal","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}